h age  and this is of great concern given the  change in demographics in the upcoming  years  and so in order to contain the  escalating increase in healthcare  spending  it is desirable to reduce the overall  burden of disability and chronic disease  and it's thought that a more  cost-effective approach would be to  target aging itself  instead of all the different  age-associated diseases separately  genetic studies and the testing of a  variety of different compounds  have shown in a variety of different  model organisms that aging can be  thought of as being adaptive and  amenable to these interventions  as they can extend both health plan and  lifespan and so the big  question is how much this information if  at all can it be translated to humans  and so this is kind of where the itp  come back in as their aim is to  take all this information that's  currently been published and to  capitalize on it by rigorously  evaluating  any potential agents that have promise  for human use  and so what do i mean by rigorously  evaluate well this comes down to the key  principles that the itp  use to make sure that they follow good  experimental design  and to improve the validity of any  results that they can get  so what are these principles well the  first principle is that they run these  tests  in parallel at three different sites so  this includes the jackson laboratories  one at the university of michigan  and one at the university of texas  health sciences center  at san antonio and so not only does the  setup mean that more expertise can be  brought into the programme  but it also provides reproducibility by  having the same interventions tested  at three different sites the next few  principles revolve around the mice that  they use in their studies  firstly they use genetically  heterogeneous mice referred to as um  het3  and so the way that they can get these  genetically heterogeneous mice is by  using a cross-breeding scheme  between different strains of females and  males and so none of the mice are  identical  but each mouse shares half of its genome  with every other mouse in the population  and so the population level diversity  can be replicated  and so the genetic heterogeneity is  reproducible  such that the experiments can be  replicated at any time by starting with  the same parental strains  and so you can think of the mice all  being siblings to each other with the  perspective of their nuclear genome  and the advantages of having genetic  heterogeneity  basically reduces the possibility that  there was any strain specific  characteristics  that might be influencing the outcome in  this case the lifespan of the mice to  the different interventions  the second principle with the mice is  that they have a lot of them  each of the sites has 44 males and 36  females in each test treatment  the reason that there are more males  than females is due to the fact that  they can often lose males due to  fighting  and then for the control group they have  twice the number of mice so  88 males and 72 females and the reason  for these numbers  is due to a protocol developed by two  statisticians  which show that groups of this size  would detect a 10  change in mean lifespan with 80 power  even if data from one of the sites was  lost because of a systematic failure  and so the idea is you get power in  numbers and then the last principle  that's worth mentioning is their  unusually collaborative programme  by which they engage the broader  research community in the search for  agents that slow aging and extend mao's  lifespan  in fact they make an annual call for  proposals to the research community  and proposals are accepted from all  scientific quarters  whether it's in the us foreign  universities commercial entities or even  individuals without  institutional affiliations and so far  the itp have received more than 150  different proposals  out of these they've whittled their  number down to 43 agents that they've  actually tested  with increased longevity being seen in  the mice in at least one of the sexes  for seven of these 43 agents  but before i mention what these seven  agents are and some of the agents that  haven't been successful in increasing  lifespan  it's worth just mentioning how the  program is actually designed  as you can see in the schematic that i'm  drawing out after evaluation of the  prospective compound that's been  proposed  chosen compounds initially enter stage 1  whereby it focuses on lifespan as the  primary endpoint  compounds that seem promising in terms  of significantly extending lifespan in  the mice  then proceed to stage 2 which follows up  positive findings from stage 1 with  additional lifespan studies and can also  include  those response studies where appropriate  as well as a wide array of health  measurements  and cross-sectional pathology analysis  now the caveat with all this is that by  focusing on lifespan measurements  and omitting most physiological  assessments from stage 1  could in principle miss discoveries or  compounds that do  promote specific health outcomes but  don't necessarily alter mortality risk  and mice  however if these additional measurements  were included in stage 1  it has the downside that it would reduce  the number of compounds that could be  tested each year  so now for the book that you're probably  most interested in what are the seven  different compounds that have shown life  extension  in the mice well i'm going to give them  to you now and  to add a bit of fun i'll give them in  the order by the strength of the  conclusions  and depth of the supporting evidence  that they have so far for each one  so we'll go from good to last good i  suppose  so first up is rapamycin and that was  shown to extend  mean and maximal longevity in both sexes  when started at either 9 months or 20  months of age  and so rapamycin is an antifungal  antibiotic that was first isolated from  an easter island soil sample  and has been approved by the fda for its  immunosuppressive and anti-rejection  properties  the properties of rapamycin are thought  to be achieved by its inhibition of the  mammalian target of rapamycin  mtor which promotes growth signaling  within a cell  with enter activity being regulated by  nutrients growth factors and the  cellular energy status  and so follow-up studies into rapamycin  were performed to see if there were any  dose dependencies whereby if higher  doses of rapamycin are more effective  and that's basically what they saw with  the highest dose they used for rapamycin  resulting in a 26 extension of the  median lifespan of females  and 23 in males and interestingly  rapamycin  also produced a dose-dependent decrease  in liver degeneration in male mice  however at the high-dose treatment of  rapamycin  it was seen in both sexes that there was  more cataract severity than control mice  and another detrimental effect of  rapamycin is testicular degeneration  which was significantly elevated by all  three doses of rapamycin  and so therefore it seems likely that  the therapeutic dose of rapomycin  will vary for different pathologies and  that evidently more research is required  to look into rapamycin or rapamycin  alternatives  known as rapalogues the second compound  that extended lifespan of both male  and female mice was a-carbose  supplementation  and so a-carbose is a drug that is  approved for the clinical treatment of  diabetes  it works by inhibiting alpha glucosidase  which therefore  inhibits intestinal digestion of  carbohydrates and so it's thought in  this manner that  a cowboys can help to lower blood  glucose levels hence the reason why it's  prescribed for diabetes  however it's not sure if it's the  blunting of these surges and blood  glucose levels that can account for the  effect on mouse lifespan  in these studies and so to investigate  this further the itp are now completing  a survival analysis of mice treated with  chemically flosin  which is also thought to boast these  glucose spikes and so i guess if there's  a benefit with this compound  it would potentially support the role of  high glucose peaks as a modulator of  aging rates in mice  which is now making me rethink that  cream egg i just ate  anyway it's important to also point out  that acaba has led to a much stronger  effect on median lifespan in males than  females  and indeed follow-up work seems to have  shown male-specific improvements in  insulin sensitivity and glucose  tolerance the next compound  also showed sex specificity only  improving lifespan in male mice  this compound is nor dihydroguaritic  acid  better referred to as ndga which is far  more easier to say  so ndga is an antioxidant compound  found in the creosote bush which has  been used in traditional medicine and  has since been investigated in the lab  and has been shown to modulate the  activity of nrf2  and also more recently shown to be able  to activate autophagy  however i think this is still a bit  under-investigated moreover the  sex-specific difference is also very  interesting  and i should also point out at this  point that all of these  survival charts can be accessed on the  mouse phenome database websites  which is where the itp will upload all  of their latest data  and so there's a variety of different  possibilities to why there could be sex  specific differences  for example it could be that the  metabolism of these compounds  vary between different genders or it  could be that male specific pathways  relating to aging are being targeted  or it could be that there are some  negative consequences  on lifespan for females as opposed to  males and the reason why i emphasize the  fact that there could just be so many  differences between  males and females is the fact that the  fourth compound that  they mention is 17 alpha estradiol which  also shows an increased male lifespan  but not female lifespan  so what is 17 alpha estradiol well it  isn't  17 beta estradiol which is the most  biologically active  and abundant estrogen in circulation  instead the alpha form  is considered a non-feminizing hormone  with reduced affinity for the estrogen  receptor  and whilst i think further studies are  required to understand the molecular  mechanism  of 17 alpha estradiol it has been shown  in follow-up studies by the itp  that metabolomic changes seen in the 17  alpha estradiol treated male mice  were in general not seen in females or  in males castrated  prior to treatment suggesting that the  drug effects require the presence of  testicular hormones  and the next compound is protandim a  patented  compound which is actually a mixture of  botanical extracts  that is thought to activate an rf2 and  i'm not really going to talk too much  about this one  partly because while we're only extended  median lifespan of males  not females but also because there  really isn't that much  known about it besides these itp studies  so  i think i'm just going to leave it at  that for now in sixth place we have food  supplemented with glycine that resulted  in a significant lifespan extension  in both males and females but the effect  in males was larger than in females  both at the median lifespan and at the  90th percentile marks  but the longevity effects in general  were quite small and did not much alter  median survival age  but the reason they tested glycine was  due to the fact that glycine  supplementation is thought to be able to  mimic  methionine restriction due to the role  that glycine plays in methionine  metabolism  and restriction of methionine was shown  to extend lifespan in rats and mice in  previous studies  and just in case you didn't know  methionine and glycine are both amino  acids  so they are building blocks of proteins  and so that finally leads on to the  seventh compound  which drumroll plays is  aspirin which is commonly used as an  anti-inflammatory agent  and that was only shown to significantly  extend male lifespan and the sex  specific differences in this case  seems to be due to the fact that females  tended to have  a higher relative rate of metabolism of  aspirin  so it's less active metabolites  salicylate  but more interestingly when these  results were replicated in male mice  with two higher doses  there wasn't a longevity benefit and so  additional studies would be needed to  understand these conflicting results  and so it's also worth mentioning a  couple of compounds that haven't shown  any lifespan extension in the mice at  the doses  used in these studies this included  curcumin  fish oil green tea extract resveratrol  and metformin although as i said earlier  you can see the full list on the mouse  phenome database website  but it is worth mentioning in the case  of metformin that when they combined  metformin with rapamycin it had an  enhanced  effect on lifespan compared to rapamycin  alone  so that just goes to show how these  studies do just have  one compound at a specific dose and that  it could be that combined with something  else could be  more beneficial and i suppose in some  ways there's kind of point holes in the  limitations of the itp study  but as i mentioned earlier there has to  be some kind of trade-off  into how they can robustly test as many  compounds as possible  with the fact that some of the compounds  may be promising but are missed off due  to the way that they  purely focus on lifespan for their  primary endpoints  and in terms of their latest data at the  time of their most recent update which  was  earlier this year there are several  stage 1 studies that were essentially  complete  or in preparation for peer review and  this included longevity data for  nicotinamide ribosides  and canoglyflosin as well as a test of  alternate dosing schemes for rapamycin  comparing continuous treatment  with periodic treatments in monthly  cycles  so it's interesting and exciting to see  what compounds  are extending lifespan and mice but how  much can this be translated to humans  well for an intervention to be  translatable it would be desirable to  satisfy  some of the following conditions  including low toxicity well actually  that would be a definite you'd want to  satisfy that  with few side effects have effective  oral administration  have effective stability and scalability  and low manufacturing costs  be detectable in blood and be effective  when administered later life or once  symptoms have already started to develop  and another consideration given that  there are already sex specific  differences  in these different compounds would be to  take into account all of the different  diet environmental  gender ethnicity polymorphisms and age  variables that make all of us unique as  this could also impact how beneficial  these compounds are  but all in all the work from the itp is  very encouraging  and is taking us that step closer to  understanding the aging process  so with that i'd like to thank the  sponsor for this video longevity  technology for which i am very grateful  longevity technology deliver high  quality daily news and insights on  research investments and technologies  that extend health span and lifespan  find the link to their website in the  description  so i hope you've learned something in  this video thank you to my patreon  supporters  and thank you for listening


